    description: "LIVE DEFECT IN MERGED CODE (PR #197, shipped 2026-08-04). Found by an\nadversarial review panel, verified independently before filing.\n\n_qualified_callee (taint.py:1150) builds the ADR-0017 section 4 catalogue\nlookup key from _extract_callee_module, which returns the RAW path slot. For an\nin-repo callee that slot is a FILE PATH WITH ITS EXTENSION:\n\n  python:src/app/views.py:10-20:handler:function\n    _extract_callee_module   -> 'src/app/views.py'\n    _qualified_callee        -> 'src/app/views.py.handler'     <- lookup key\n    _module_from_symbol_path -> 'src/app/views'                <- the normalizer\n\n_module_from_symbol_path (taint.py:1216) exists PRECISELY to strip that\nextension -- it was added by WI-damir for exactly this class of bug -- and the\nnew code does not use it. So no first-party callee can ever match a catalogue\nentry, and the \"first-party in-repo callee\" bucket in the section 4 enumeration\n(9.8% of 264 escape sites) was produced by a key that cannot match anything.\n\nTHIS IS L53 IN PRODUCTION CODE, WRITTEN BY THE AGENT WHO HAD JUST CITED L53\nFIVE TIMES IN THE SAME SESSION. The rule is \"when a production classification\nexists for the thing you are computing, computing it yourself IS the bug\". A\nsecond naive parse of the colon-tolerant id grammar is the same defect\n_symbol_path_slot's header warns about, and the new function's own docstring\nclaims it is \"built from the two existing production extractors rather than a\nfresh parse\" -- which is true of the NAME half and false of the MODULE half.\n\nSECOND, SEPARATE MISS -- the shipped python catalogue is largely unreachable.\npython_stdlib.yaml declares logging.Logger.debug/info/warning/error/exception,\nwhich need module slot 'logging.Logger'. Real ids carry slot 'logging':\n    _qualified_callee(\"python:logging:0-0:info:external_symbol\") -> 'logging.info'\n    'logging.info' in catalogue -> FALSE\n    'logging.Logger.info' in catalogue -> TRUE\nSo five of the six Python TERMINATING entries can never fire. Whether the\nreceiver hint ever upgrades the slot to logging.Logger is unmeasured; the panel\nreports _resolve_receiver_hint returns None when the binding RHS is a call\nexpression, which `logger = logging.getLogger(__name__)` is.\n\nTHIRD, a silent drop worth a diagnostic: the qualified-key filter\n`{k: v for k, v in ... if k == v.function}` (taint.py:2002) discards 44 of 108\nloaded keys with no log line. The filter is correct and deliberate -- the alias\nindex is dangerous here -- but a silent 40% drop should say so.\n\nIMPACT. No verdict moved, because the section 4 result cannot reach a verdict\nat all today (see the call site note below), so this is not a wrong answer\nshipped to a user. It is a capability that was measured as ineffective when\npart of the reason was that its lookup key was malformed. Every \"section 4\nbuys nothing\" number I recorded must be re-measured after this is fixed.\n\nFIX: use _module_from_symbol_path for the module half; add a test with an\nin-repo callee id; re-run the enumeration and the catalogue-coverage numbers.\nAlso decide whether the python logging entries should be keyed on the bare\nmodule, and whether an unresolved receiver should be catalogued at all."  # 53da
    fields:  # 53da
      statement: "The ADR-0017 section 4 summary lookup key for a callee must equal the key a catalogue entry declares. For in-repo callees it currently embeds the source file extension, so no first-party callee can ever match."  # 53da
      root_cause: "_qualified_callee composes _extract_callee_module (raw path slot, extension included) with _extract_callee_name, instead of using _module_from_symbol_path, the production normalizer added by WI-damir to strip exactly that. A second naive parse of the colon-tolerant id grammar where a production classifier already existed (L53)."  # 53da
